spec:
  grid: 6-13 rows x 6-13 cols, square, black background
  shape_pairs: 1-5 pairs; each pair is one colored template + one blue placeholder with identical normalized 4-connected pixel mask
  templates:
    colors: non-blue, non-black; 1-4 distinct colors; a color may have multiple objects with different shapes
    pixel_count: 2-6 per object
    bbox: max 3x3; observed shapes include 2x1, 2x2, 2x3, 3x2, 3x3
    shapes: all distinct across templates (no two templates share a normalized mask)
  blue_placeholders:
    same set of normalized shapes as templates, placed at different positions
  layout: objects non-overlapping, min Manhattan gap >= 2 between any two objects
  output: each blue object recolored to the color of its shape-matching template; everything else unchanged

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose grid size N in [6..13], make NxN grid
    2. Choose K shape pairs in [1..5]; pick K distinct small 4-connected polyomino shapes (2-6 cells, bbox <= 3x3)
    3. Assign each shape a template color (non-blue, non-black); a color may repeat across shapes but all shapes must be distinct
    4. Place all 2K objects (K templates + K blue copies) on the grid with no overlap and min Manhattan gap >= 2
    5. Grid size must be large enough: roughly N >= 6 + (K-1)*2 to fit objects comfortably
    6. Input = grid with templates in their assigned colors and copies in blue; output = same grid with blue replaced by matched template colors
```
